itation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rush</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Cooper</surname><given-names>PR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Symptom resolution, tumor control, and side effects following postoperative radiotherapy for pituitary macroadenomas</article-title><source>Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys</source><year>1997</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>1031</fpage><lpage>1034</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9169809</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B31"><label>31</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dekkers</surname><given-names>OM</given-names></name><name><surname>van der Klaauw</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Pereira</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Quality of life is decreased after treatment for nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma</article-title><source>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</source><year>2006</year><volume>91</volume><fpage>3364</fpage><lpage>3369</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16787991</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Local control analysis of 60 patients with pituitary adenoma according to hormone-secreting status. Local control rate was significantly higher in non-secreting tumor subgroup (p = 0.042).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Cause-specific survival of 60 patients including both non-secreting and secreting tumor groups. The 10 year cause-specific survival was 98%. No statistically significant difference was found between secreting tumor group and non-secreting tumor group.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Overall survival of 60 patients including both non-secreting and secreting tumor groups. The 10 year overall survival was 93%. No statistically significant difference was found between secreting tumor group and non-secreting tumor group.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-g003"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Patient characteristics</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>Values are presented as number (%) or median (range).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Treatment methods</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i002"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Evaluation of symptoms</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i003"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Univarite analysis for local control</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i004"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T5" position="float"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Univarite analysis for local control</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i005"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T6" position="float"><label>Table 6</label><caption><p>Radiotherapy (RT)-related complications</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="roj-29-156-i006"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>OP, operation; TSA, trans-sphenoidal approach; DID, dead of intercurrent disease; CVA, cerebrovascular accident; DOC, dead of complication; ICH, intracranial hemorrhage; NED, no evidence of disease.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>